'''temporary''' (tem′pọ̄-rā̤-ri), ''a.'' [= F. ''temporaire'' = Sp. Pg. ''temporario'', < L. ''temporarius'', lasting but for a time, < ''tempus'' (''tempor-''), time, season: see ''temporal''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. Lasting for a time only; existing or continuing for a limited time; not permanent.

{{smaller block|These ''temporary'' truces and peaces were soone made and soone broken.
{{right|''Bacon'', Hist. Hen. VII., p. 191.}}}}

{{smaller block|I am satisfied, that, as we grow older, we learn to look upon our bodies more and more as a ''temporary'' possession, and less and less as identified with ourselves.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, viii.}}}}

2†. Contemporary; of the period. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|This excellent little piece ["Devil upon Two Sticks"], though it admits of some ''temporary'' strokes, such as the ridicule on the college of physicians, the political doctor, &c., yet exhibits them worked up in so brilliant and general a manner as to be always new.
{{right|''W. Cooke'', S. Foote, I. 83.}}}}

'''Temporary administrator.''' {{smaller|Same as ''special administrator'' (which see, under ''special'').}}—'''Temporary allegiance.''' {{smaller|See ''allegiance'', 1.}}—'''Temporary cartilage.''' {{smaller|See ''cartilage''.}}—'''Temporary excise.''' {{smaller|See ''Act of the Hereditary Excise'', under ''excise''<sup>2</sup>.}}—'''Temporary hours.''' {{smaller|See ''hour''.}}—'''Temporary injunction.''' {{smaller|See ''ad interim injunction'', under ''injunction''.}}—'''Temporary star''', {{smaller|a star which bursts in a few days into great brilliancy, and after some weeks or months sinks into lasting dimness.}} = '''Syn.''' {{smaller|1. ''Temporary, Temporal'' (see ''temporal''<sup>1</sup>), transient, fleeting, transitory, ephemeral, evanescent, brief.}}

'''temporisation, temporise''', etc. See ''temporization'', etc.

'''temporist†''' (tem′pọ̄-rist), ''n.'' [< L. ''tempus'' (''tempor-''), time, season, + ''-ist''.] A temporizer.

{{smaller block|Why turn a ''temporist'', row with the tide?
{{right|''Marston.''}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''temporization''' (tem″pō-ri-zā′shọn), ''n.'' [= F. ''temporisation'' = Pg. ''temporização''; as ''temporize'' + ''-ation''.] The act of temporizing; time-serving. Also spelled ''temporisation''.

{{smaller block|He [Graunt] allows that suspicions and charges of ''temporization'' and compliance had somewhat sullied his reputation.
{{right|''Johnson'', Ascham.}}}}

'''temporize''' (tem′pō-rīz), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''temporized'', ppr. ''temporizing''. [= F. ''temporiser'' = Sp. Pg. ''temporizar'' = It. ''temporeggiare''; as L. ''tempus'' (''tempor-''), time, season, + ''-ize''.] 1. To comply with the time or occasion, or with the desires of another; yield temporarily or ostensibly to the current of opinion or circumstances.

{{smaller block|The Dauphin is too wilful-opposite,
And will not ''temporize'' with my entreaties;
He flatly says he'll not lay down his arms.
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, v. 2. 125.}}
'Twas then no time her grievance to reveal,
"He 's mad who takes a lion by the ears."
This knew the Queen, and this well know the wise,
This must they learn that rightly ''temporize''.
{{right|''Drayton'', Barons' Wars, i. 36.}}}}

2†. To parley.

{{smaller block|For that he could not brook to ''temporise''
With humours maskèd in those times' disguise.
{{right|''Ford'', Fame's Memorial.}}
All these ''temporize'' with other for necessities, but all as vncertaine as peace or warres.
{{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, I. 128.}}}}

3. To dilly-dally; delay; procrastinate.

{{smaller block|The Earle of Lincolne, deceived of his hopes of the Countries concourse unto him (in which case he would have ''temporized''), . . . resolved . . . to give him [the king] battaile.
{{right|''Bacon'', Hist. Hen. VII., p. 43.}}}}

