'''tempre†''', ''v.'' A Middle English form of ''temper''.

'''tempre†, tempree†''', ''a.'' [ME., < OF. ''tempre'', < L. ''temperatus'', temperate: see ''temperate'', ''a.'' Cf. ''attempre'', ''a.''] Temperate.

{{smaller block|But the Contree where he duellethe in most comounly is in Gaydo or in Jong, that is a gode Contree and a ''tempree'' aftre that the Contree is there; but to men of this Contree it were to passyng hoot.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 240.}}}}

{{smaller block|Now had the ''tempre'' sonne al that relevyd.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Good Women (1st version), l. 116.}}}}

{{smaller block|[The later version reads ''atempre''.]}}

'''temprely†''', ''adv.'' [ME. ''temprely'', ''temperelly''; < ''tempre'', ''a.'', + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Temperately.

{{smaller block|Governeth yow also of youre diete
Al ''temperelly'', and namely in this hete.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Shipman's Tale (Harl. MS.), l. 262.}}}}

'''temprure†''', ''n.'' See ''temperure''.

'''temps<sup>1</sup>''' (F. pron. toṅ), ''n.'' 1†. See ''tense''<sup>1</sup>.—2. Specifically, in ''legerdemain'', the right opportunity for executing a required movement. {{smaller|This is gained by some act which distracts the attention of the audience while the trick is being done.}}

'''temps<sup>2</sup>†, tempse†''', ''n.'' See ''temse''.

'''tempt''' (tempt), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''tempten'' (pp. sometimes ''temped''), < OF. ''tempter'', ''tenter'', ''tanter'', F.

readings.c3s1:
tenter = Pr. ''temptar'' = Sp. Pg. ''tentar'' = It. ''tentare'', tempt, < L. ''tentare'', handle, touch, try, test, tempt (also in form ''temptare'', not a reg. variant, and explainable only as an ancient error due to some confusion; cf. E. ''daunt'', < OF. ''daunter'', ''dompter'', < L. ''domitare'', etc.), freq. of ''tenere'', pp. ''tentus'', hold: see ''tenant''. Cf. ''attempt'', etc.] '''1.''' To put to trial; try; test; put to the test. [Archaic.]

{{smaller block|Sothli he seide this thing, ''temptinge'' him; forsoth he wiste what he was to doynge.
{{right|''Wyclif'', John vi. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Tempte'' hem frist on werkes smale,
In ereed lande the plough as for to hale.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 132.}}}}

{{smaller block|God did ''tempt'' Abraham.
{{right|Gen. xxii. 1.}}}}

'''2.''' To entice; attract; allure; invite; induce; incline; dispose; incite.

{{smaller block|I am a weak one,
Arm'd only with my fears: I beseech your grace
''Tempt'' me no further.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, iii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|Still his strength conceal'd,
Which ''tempted'' our attempt, and wrought our fall.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 642.}}}}

{{smaller block|It was now that he began to ''tempt'' me about writing "the Dutch War."
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Feb. 13, 1669.}}}}

{{smaller block|Green covered places ''tempted'' the foot, and black bog-holes discouraged it.
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Lorna Doone, lix.}}}}

'''3.''' To incite or entice to evil; entice to something wrong by presenting arguments that are plausible or convincing, or by the offer of some pleasure or apparent advantage as the inducement; seduce.

{{smaller block|Thus deuelis ther wilis caste}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|If the parliament were as ''temptable'' as any other assembly, the managers must fail for want of tools to work with.
{{right|''Swift.''}}}}

'''temptableness''' (temp′tạ-bl-nes), ''n.'' The character of being temptable; temptability.

'''temptation''' (temp-tā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ME. ''temptacioun'', < OF. ''temptacion'', ''tentation'', F. ''tentation'' = Pr. ''temptacio'', ''tentacio'' = Sp. ''tentacion'' = Pg. ''tentação'' = It. ''tentazione'', < L. ''tentatio(n-)'', trial, temptation, < ''tentare'', try, test, tempt: see ''tempt''.] 1. The act of testing or trying; trial. [Archaic.]

{{smaller block|Or hath God assayed to go and take him a nation from the midst of another nation, by ''temptations'', by signs, and by wonders?
{{right|Deut. iv. 34.}}}}

{{smaller block|A ''temptation'' is only another word for an experiment, or trial; a trial whether we will do or forbear such a thing.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', Sermons, II. iv.}}}}

2. Enticement to evil, as by specious argument, flattery, or the offer of some real or apparent good.

{{smaller block|Most dangerous<br>Is that ''temptation'' that doth goad us on<br>To sin in loving virtue.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., ii. 2. 182.}}}}

{{smaller block|He who resigns the world has no ''temptation'' to envy, hatred, malice, anger.
{{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 282.}}}}

{{smaller block|He drilled himself till inflexible habit stood sentinel before all those postern-weaknesses which temperament leaves unbolted to ''temptation''.
{{right|''Lowell'', Cambridge Thirty Years Ago.}}}}

3. The state of being tempted, or enticed to evil.
